" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133306337,imgsize-66136.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Indian-origin grandmother with Green Card detained by ICE after living in the US for 30 years. Her daughter is an immigration attorney]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133305137.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/indian-origin-grandmother-with-green-card-detained-by-ice-after-living-in-the-us-for-30-years-her-daughter-is-an-immigration-attorney/articleshow/133305137.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/indian-origin-grandmother-with-green-card-detained-by-ice-after-living-in-the-us-for-30-years-her-daughter-is-an-immigration-attorney/articleshow/133305137.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-18T00:11:37+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Poulomi Ghosh</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133305252,imgsize-84396.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Meet Mohammed Almuntasir: Libyan photographer's video proves "ghost cat" still lives]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133292538.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In a groundbreaking 2017 video, a Libyan photographer captured the elusive sand cats, marking the first sighting of these fascinating creatures in their natural habitat. This revelation sparked an extensive eight-year research collaboration between zoologist Firas Hayder and photographer Mohammed Almuntasir, who combined their expertise and local insights to gather essential data. Their ongoing efforts are instrumental in enhancing our understanding of sand cat populations in Libya.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/meet-mohammed-almuntasir-the-libyan-wildlife-photographer-whose-18-second-youtube-video-proved-a-ghost-cat-still-lives-in-libya/articleshow/133292538.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/meet-mohammed-almuntasir-the-libyan-wildlife-photographer-whose-18-second-youtube-video-proved-a-ghost-cat-still-lives-in-libya/articleshow/133292538.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-18T02:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133292740,imgsize-1277383.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[In 1745, the US welcomed mimosa trees for pink flowers; they later spread invasively]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133292086.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Mimosa, introduced to the United States around 1745, quickly became a sought-after ornamental tree. However, it broke free from cultivation, proliferating in disturbed environments throughout the southern and eastern U.S. Its rapid growth and abundance of seeds have turned it into an invasive species, vying with native flora and reshaping ecosystems. Effective management must focus on both current plants and their future offspring to mitigate this ongoing issue.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1745-the-us-welcomed-mimosa-trees-for-their-pink-flowers-they-later-escaped-gardens-and-spread-as-an-invasive-species/articleshow/133292086.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1745-the-us-welcomed-mimosa-trees-for-their-pink-flowers-they-later-escaped-gardens-and-spread-as-an-invasive-species/articleshow/133292086.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-17T21:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133292175,imgsize-1807779.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[After 19 years, a beaver returned to a California creek, reshaping its habitat]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133289963.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Following a devastating fire in 2016 that impacted Strawberry Creek, Great Basin National Park staff noticed signs of beaver activity prompting restoration initiatives in 2021. By constructing artificial dams, the project significantly enhanced the creek's ecosystem and led to a remarkable increase in trout populations. In 2024, a beaver was spotted for the first time in nearly two decades, symbolizing nature's resilience and recovery after human efforts.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-2024-a-beaver-returned-to-a-california-creek-after-19-years-and-began-cutting-down-aspen-trees-beside-a-man-made-dam/articleshow/133289963.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-2024-a-beaver-returned-to-a-california-creek-after-19-years-and-began-cutting-down-aspen-trees-beside-a-man-made-dam/articleshow/133289963.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-17T21:20:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133290070,imgsize-1357380.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Argentina’s mysterious floating island named ‘EL Ojo’ that appears to rotate on its own]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133298159.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/argentinas-mysterious-floating-island-named-el-ojo-that-appears-to-rotate-on-its-own-in-perfect-circular-motion/arti